WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
necodoc: from the obligee's PDF to a renewal run.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.667
Start from the obligee's bond form, exactly as they publish it.

3
00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:09.333
AI proposes a box for every blank, with a name and a type.

4
00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:12.500
A person confirms them in the overlay editor, with a live preview.

5
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.167
Designed templates carry their own rules: this part shows only when it applies.

6
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:19.500
Tick “Add a co-principal” and the bond names a second party and their signature block.

7
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.500
Whoever fills it in gets a guided form, one step at a time.

8
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.500
Finish, and the PDF is ready, from the same renderer as the preview.

9
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.500
Or call it from your policy system: HTML and data in, PDF out.

10
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.500
109 ms median for a 3-page invoice with header and footer, measured.

11
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.167
Month-end renewals: one batch of 1,000 rows.

12
00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:39.500
Add workers to go faster: 1,230 documents a minute on 8 workers, 944 on one. No licence fee per worker.

13
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.833
E-signature ready: each PDF comes with its signers and where every signature goes.

14
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:45.500
Hand the package to DocuSign, Dropbox Sign or Adobe Acrobat Sign.

15
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.167
Every change and sign-in lands in an audit log that can't be edited.

16
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:50.333
Every render is logged with its input, output and timing.

17
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:52.500
It runs in your cloud. Files stay in your S3-compatible storage or Azure Blob.

18
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.500
necodoc. Self-hosted, open source under MIT. github.com/mikevalstar/necodoc
